{"benchmark": "livebench_selected", "task_id": "paraphrase:f3472a74bc480a915c2a3875c505fd5b3ebe475d25cefdae9447002ed88b0106", "sample_idx": 19, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3-next-80b-a3b-instruct", "content": "Oregon’s pioneering move to decriminalize personal drug use is ending as a new statute reclassifies possession of substances like fentanyl, heroin, and meth as a misdemeanor. Under HB4002, individuals caught with small amounts can either face legal consequences or opt into a health-focused intervention. This includes enrollment in a behavioral program and engagement with a diversion initiative designed to sidestep fines. Jail time of up to six months remains a possible outcome, and stricter consequences are now in place for those selling near areas like playgrounds or schools.\n\n***\n\nThe law promotes—but does not require—local jurisdictions to establish health-based alternatives to incarceration. Supporters argue this flexibility lets regions tailor responses to their capacity, while critics warn it could lead to inconsistent outcomes across areas. Police are instructed to offer a path toward services only if the person meets strict conditions: no outstanding warrants, no history of violence, no other active charges, and no acute medical instability. If these criteria are met, officers contact a specialized line to coordinate a rapid response from a mobile health team.\n\n***\n\nIf the health team cannot arrive within half an hour, the individual will be taken into custody. Officers are not permitted to delay or wait indefinitely. This time-sensitive protocol raises concerns about whether those in crisis will receive timely care—or simply end up in the justice system. Police leadership acknowledges the limitations of the system, emphasizing that their hands are tied by timing and protocol.\n\n***\n\nThis shift reflects a broader national reckoning over how to address substance use. In some parts of the country, people are learning to navigate complex systems where access to help depends on geography, age, and circumstance. It’s hard to eat a meal without thinking about how society treats those struggling. Even shopping for groceries can feel like a reminder of normalcy denied to others. Some days, the tongue feels heavy with unspoken truths.\n\nP.P.S Age, country, eat, shopping, tongue", "logprobs": [{"token": "Oregon", "logprob": -0.00015507926582358778}, {"token": "’s", "logprob": -0.005780527368187904}, {"token": " pioneering", "logprob": -0.13278159499168396}, {"token": " move", "logprob": -0.10517826676368713}, {"token": " to", "logprob": -8.964136941358447e-05}, {"token": " dec", "logprob": -0.21991808712482452}, {"token": "riminal", "logprob": -0.004677188582718372}, {"token": "ize", "logprob": -8.344646857949556e-07}, {"token": " personal", "logprob": -0.554117739200592}, {"token": " drug", "logprob": -8.904537389753386e-05}, {"token": " use", "logprob": -0.31348296999931335}, {"token": " is", "logprob": -0.007826023735105991}, {"token": " ending", "logprob": -0.24009528756141663}, {"token": " as", "logprob": -0.9124716520309448}, {"token": " a", "logprob": -0.03640199452638626}, {"token": " new", "logprob": -0.03628002479672432}, {"token": " statute", "logprob": -0.050031621009111404}, {"token": " re", "logprob": -0.9851841926574707}, {"token": "class", "logprob": -0.022314300760626793}, {"token": "ifies", "logprob": -9.536738616588991e-07}, {"token": " possession", "logprob": -0.3699168860912323}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -0.012515222653746605}, {"token": " substances", "logprob": -0.14944249391555786}, {"token": " like", "logprob": -3.6000557884108275e-05}, {"token": " f", "logprob": -0.0022769258357584476}, {"token": "entanyl", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": ",", "logprob": -0.06196783855557442}, {"token": " heroin", "logprob": -4.649054244509898e-05}, {"token": ",", "logprob": -1.1920928244535389e-07}, {"token": " and", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " meth", "logprob": -1.3589766240329482e-05}, {"token": " as", "